---
import FlowShot from '../FlowShot.astro';
import FlowDetail from '../FlowDetail.astro';
import FlowShot from '../../../components/flow/FlowShot.astro';
import FlowDetail from '../../../components/flow/FlowDetail.astro';
import shotSmartRouting from '../../../assets/flow/shots/flow-smart-routing-shot.webp';
import detailRoutingDecided from '../../../assets/flow/details/detail-routing-decided.webp';
import detailRoutingRules from '../../../assets/flow/details/detail-routing-rules.webp';
import detailRoutingNorule from '../../../assets/flow/details/detail-routing-norule.webp';
import detailRoutingProviders from '../../../assets/flow/details/detail-routing-providers.webp';
---
{/* Chapter 4c: Smart Routing. Written 2026-08-18 from the smart-routing
    brief (flow-smart-routing-source.md). Sits directly after Benchmarks
    because that is the argument's own order: which of your models is
    fastest (4b), and who decides which one runs each request (4c). The
    speed rule literally reads the Benchmarks screen's own measurements,
    so the two chapters share evidence, not just adjacency.

    VOCABULARY RULE, non-negotiable. The implementation says predicates,
    resolvers, gates and fail-closed. None of those may appear here. The
    published vocabulary is the screen's: rules, "rules decide in order;
    the first one that matches applies", Deciding, models qualify,
    provider routing order. The feature is SMART ROUTING; the name "Auto
    routing" was retired 2026-08-18 and must not be reintroduced anywhere,
    including alt text.

    Guardrails from the brief that must not be softened OR strengthened:
      * It is a deterministic ordered rulebook. Never "AI-powered
        routing", never "learns your preferences". The refusal to adapt
        is the selling point.
      * Rules see locality, document fit, price, and measured speed.
        NEVER a quality verdict; the Benchmarks chapter above carries the
        same boundary from the other side.
      * Claim system-wide rules only. Folder- and document-scoped rule
        sets exist in the mechanism but no surface edits them yet.
      * The speed rule ranks only models measured on THIS Mac; unmeasured
        models are not ranked at all. A cost rule cannot admit a model
        with no published price, because Flow will not guess a rate.

    STAGE CORNER: --br (was --bl on the single page; 2026-08-20 split made Domains' --bl the adjacent stage). Pinned by both neighbours: the previous stage is
    Benchmarks' --br and the next is Chapter 7's --tr (Chapters 5 and 6
    carry no stage). --bl also lands on the emptiest region of this
    capture, below the provider list. tests/e2e/product-stage.spec.ts
    asserts adjacency in rendered order, so re-check the ADJACENT stages
    before changing this. */}
<section class="border-t border-border/60 px-6 py-14 sm:py-20" aria-labelledby="tour-routing">
  <div>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      <p class="of-eyebrow" data-animate="rise">Smart Routing</p>
      <h3 id="tour-routing" class="of-display scroll-mt-28 mt-4 text-[clamp(1.85rem,3.4vw,2.75rem)]" data-animate="lead">
        Your rules pick the model. The screen names the rule that did.
      </h3>
      <p class="of-lede mt-5" data-animate="rise">
        Flow can reach eight providers across four domains, so every AI action starts with the
        same question: which model should do this work? Smart Routing answers it with rules you
        keep. A short, ordered list, read top to bottom: rules decide in order, and the first
        one that matches applies. Five rules come built in, named in plain words, from
        "Stays on this Mac" to "Cheapest". Duplicate any of them and edit your own copy.
      </p>
      <p class="of-lede mt-4" data-animate="rise">
        Elsewhere, routing is an auto tier: the vendor's black box picks a model by its own
        economics and tells you afterwards, if at all. Flow's routing is a rulebook you wrote,
        applied in your order, decided on your Mac. The screen states the whole procedure in
        one sentence: rules decide in order, and the first one that matches applies.
      </p>
    </div>

    {/* The corner crop is the rulebook itself: four ordered rules, the
        Deciding badge, live counts, and the orange no-model state. The
        short "decided by" strip could not take this corner: at 82 source
        pixels tall it renders shorter than the stage overhang and never
        overlaps the shot (product-stage.spec.ts catches exactly that), so
        it sits below as its own centred figure, machine-strip style. */}
    <div class="of-stage of-stage--pad-bottom mt-12" data-animate="plate">
      <FlowShot
        src={shotSmartRouting}
        alt="Orionfold Flow's Smart Routing settings beside an annual operating review: the Allow Smart Routing switch naming the routed model and its deciding rule, four ordered rules with live counts, and the provider routing order below."
        crop
        sizes="(min-width: 1280px) 78rem, (min-width: 1024px) 64rem, 100vw"
      />
      <FlowDetail
        class="of-stage__detail of-stage__detail--br"
        src={detailRoutingRules}
        alt="Flow's Smart Routing rules: the sentence 'Rules decide in order; the first one that matches applies', then four numbered rules with reorder arrows. Rule 1, Can hold the whole document, wears the Deciding badge above 4 of 8 models qualify. Rule 4, Can hold the entire archive, reads No model qualifies right now in orange."
        caption="Rules decide in order · The first one that matches applies"
        sizes="(min-width: 1280px) 30rem, (min-width: 1024px) 27rem, calc(100vw - 3rem)"
        animate
      />
    </div>

    {/* The signed route, alone and centred. Same shape problem as the
        benchmarks machine strip: a wide, short ribbon that letterboxes at
        full column width, so it is capped near the copy measure and the
        sentence stays legible. It gets its own moment because it is the
        chapter's headline claim: the route is not just a result, it is
        signed by the rule that picked it. */}
    <figure class="routing-decided mx-auto mt-14" data-animate="rise">
      <FlowDetail
        src={detailRoutingDecided}
        alt="Flow's Allow Smart Routing switch, turned on, above the sentence: Smart Routing would use qwen3.5-35b-a3b-4bit. Decided by the rule 'Can hold the whole document'."
        caption="The route, signed · One sentence names the model and the rule that picked it"
        sizes="(min-width: 900px) 38rem, calc(100vw - 3rem)"
      />
    </figure>
    <p class="routing-decided__note mx-auto mt-5 text-center" data-animate="rise">
      Wherever the route is shown, the deciding rule is named in one plain sentence:
      <em>Decided by the rule "Can hold the whole document".</em> The toolbar's hover panel
      and the Settings screen read that sentence from one shared place, so the two can never
      tell you different stories.
    </p>

    {/* Two coupled claims, same layout as the Benchmarks chapter for the
        same legibility reason (.bench-honesty below): each crop is a wide
        row of small UI text and needs the full column to stay readable.
        Order is deliberate: what the rulebook refuses to pretend, then
        the provider order underneath it all. */}
    <div class="bench-honesty mx-auto mt-16">
      <div class="bench-honesty__item">
        <div class="bench-honesty__copy" data-animate="rise">
          <h4 class="bench-honesty__head">A rule that cannot deliver says so. In orange.</h4>
          <p class="bench-honesty__body">
            A rule that matches but admits no model is not skipped in silence. Its row says
            "No model qualifies right now.", in orange, and the decision moves on to the next
            rule where you can watch it happen. The refusals run all the way down. The speed
            rule ranks only by speeds measured on this Mac, the Benchmarks screen's own
            numbers, so a model that was never measured is not ranked at all. And a cost rule
            will not guess: a model with no published price simply cannot qualify.
          </p>
        </div>
        <FlowDetail
          src={detailRoutingNorule}
          alt="Three of Flow's routing rules: Fastest measured on this Mac reading Would try qwen3.5-35b-a3b-4bit first, Stays on this Mac with 5 of 8 models qualify, and Can hold the entire archive with the orange line No model qualifies right now."
          caption="Refusal over pretense · An empty match is stated, never skipped"
          sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 62rem, calc(100vw - 3rem)"
        />
      </div>
      <div class="bench-honesty__item">
        <div class="bench-honesty__copy" data-animate="rise">
          <h4 class="bench-honesty__head">Providers try in your order, and the off ones stay visible.</h4>
          <p class="bench-honesty__body">
            Below the rules sits the provider routing order: all eight providers Flow can
            reach, in the order you set. Providers you have not enabled are labeled
            "Not enabled" rather than dropped from the list, because a provider that silently
            vanished would leave you guessing why a route changed. Rules pick from what these
            providers can offer right now, never from a stored claim about what they once had.
          </p>
        </div>
        <FlowDetail
          src={detailRoutingProviders}
          alt="Flow's provider routing order: eight providers from Flow Runtime to Claude Code in a numbered, reorderable list, with Codex CLI and Claude Code labeled Not enabled."
          caption="All eight, always listed · Off means labeled, not hidden"
          sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 62rem, calc(100vw - 3rem)"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <ul class="flow-list mx-auto mt-14 max-w-3xl">
      <li>Five rules ship built in and read-only: Stays on this Mac, Can hold the whole document, Under $0.05 a run, Fastest measured on this Mac, and Cheapest. Duplicate one to make your own editable copy.</li>
      <li>Every rule row carries a live count, like "4 of 8 models qualify.", and the rule currently choosing wears a Deciding badge. A new rule joins at the end of the order, so adding one never quietly outranks the rules you already trust.</li>
      <li>The rule editor names every problem beside the field it belongs to, and Save stays off until the draft is valid. A broken rule cannot be saved now and fail you quietly later.</li>
      <li>Every surface prices a rule at the open document's own size, so a cost rule cannot route one way while you hover one action and another way on the next.</li>
      <li>Pin a model yourself and the rules section says the rules decide nothing. No rule takes credit for a choice you made by hand.</li>
      <li>Rules see where a model runs, what it can hold, what it costs, and how fast it measured on this Mac. They never judge answer quality, because Flow has not measured that.</li>
      <li>Nothing here learns, weights, or decides off this Mac. The same rules in the same order pick the same model, and you can read why, every time.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="of-spec-fine mx-auto mt-8 max-w-3xl text-center uppercase tracking-[0.14em]">
      Captured 2026-08-18 on the running build. Every sentence in these pictures is the screen's own, photographed rather than promised.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
